/// `interop-boundary` is the boundary with Rust: a gandr definition called from Rust, and a Rust function called from gandr.
///
/// Implemented: a Rust caller holding a composed program calls a definition by name on values it builds and reads the answer back into its own arena; the arguments are applied in order, an answered function is left unforced, and a refused definition is unrunnable before a run.
/// Not implemented: values crossing by Rust's default layout, `Option<&u32>` by its niche and a list in two layouts agreeing after transport; an `extern` declaration admitted against its function row when it is declared (#2901).
///
/// The Rust caller is `crates/surface-dispatcher/tests/boundary.rs`; each call below is one it makes.
///
/// - see: corpus/model/interop/boundary/round-trip.gandr

// `double` and `less`.
import "../../model/interop/boundary/round-trip.gandr";

/// `double` at 21, the call the Rust caller makes first.
@[ runs("42") ]
def answer() -> Integer { double(21) }

/// `less` at 7 and 19: the order the Rust caller passes decides the sign.
@[ runs("-12") ]
def reversed() -> Integer { less(7, 19) }
